      "markdown": "## Linear Transformations (continued)\n\nIf A in (11) is square, $n \\times n$, then (11) maps $R^{n}$ into $R^{n}$. If this A is nonsingular, so that $A^{-1}$ exists (see Sec. 7.8), then multiplication of (11) by $A^{-1}$ from the left and use of $A^{-1}A = I$ gives the inverse transformation\n\n $$ \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\mathbf{y}. $$ \n\nIt maps every y = y_{0} onto that x, which by (11) is mapped onto y_{0}. The inverse of a linear transformation is itself linear, because it is given by a matrix, as (14) shows.",
